+## Off-host proof — a CI job that proves a must-pass criterion
+
+Some must-pass criteria cannot be checked on the verifying host at all (read-aloud needs host
+audio, for example), yet a CI job does prove them. The success-definition table may carry an
+optional last column, `Off-host proof`, **after `Must pass`**, naming that job for a criterion:
+
+```markdown
+| ID | Functional statement | Cites | Evidence that would prove it | Derivation | Tier 1 | Parts | Must pass | Off-host proof |
+|----|----------------------|-------|------------------------------|------------|--------|-------|-----------|----------------|
+| FS-4 | The passage is read aloud | §2 line 9 | audio capture of the passage | [read] | judge-only — audio | | O3 | .github/workflows/ci.yml#read-aloud |
+```
+
+- **Where it comes from.** The TRD author declares the job at plan time, in the optional
+  `Off-host proof` column of the TRD's `## Objectives` table, on a must-pass objective (see the
+  TRD-authoring contract). **The deriver copies that objective's cell onto every criterion row
+  whose `Must pass` cell names the objective**, verbatim, and writes nothing else in the column:
+  it never invents a job, never writes a cell on a row that names no such objective, and leaves
+  the column out entirely when no objective it was given carries a cell. On the spec path,
+  `spec-scope.js criteria` makes the same copy deterministically.
+- **The cell.** One CI job as `<workflow file>#<job name>`, for example
+  `.github/workflows/ci.yml#read-aloud`; blank, `no`, `none`, `n/a` or a dash means none.
+- **Who reads it.** One deterministic reader in `functional-verification.js`
+  (`off-host-proofs`, beside `must-pass-coverage`) returns the declarations keyed by criterion
+  id, with the cell split into its workflow file and job name, **only for must-pass rows**. A
+  cell on any other row, a malformed cell, or one that names a different job from the one its
+  objective declares in the TRD is returned as `ignored` with a reason and never acted on.
+- **Absent means today.** A definition with no `Off-host proof` column, or a row with a blank
+  cell, behaves exactly as it did before this column existed. A check row leaves it blank.
+- **What it does.** Nothing at derivation time. At run time the CI job's conclusion for the
+  run's commit is looked up before the workflow is dispatched; only a green job settles a
+  must-pass criterion the host cannot check (kind `not-checkable-here`) as `met`, citing the
+  run. A `build-defect` or `evidence-gap` criterion is never settled by it.
+
+---
+
